course: dictionary
lesson: 686
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. Baltic with a capital B names a place: the Baltic Sea in northern Europe. In everyday talk, «It's Baltic!» means it is freezing cold.
explain: «The Baltic Sea is brackish, much less salty than the ocean.» The clues are the sea and the ocean, so Baltic is a place here, not a feeling about the weather.
explain: «It's Baltic out there, so zip your coat up.» Nobody is talking about a sea here. The words out there and zip your coat up tell you Baltic means very cold. This slang is used in British, Irish and Scottish speech.
explain: Always ask: which meaning fits the sentence? Put your meaning in place of the word and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Baltic is a name, so it begins with a capital letter: the Baltic Sea, the Baltic states.
fact: The Baltic Sea is in northern Europe, and the Baltic states are Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania.
fact: Baltic amber and Baltic languages, such as Latvian and Lithuanian, come from that same region.
fact: «It's Baltic!» is UK slang that means it is freezing cold.
fact: Context clues can be place names, like the ocean, or weather words, like zip your coat up.
exercise: «The Baltic Sea is brackish, much less salty than the ocean.» In this sentence, what is the Baltic? | *A sea in northern Europe | A cold, grey wind | A kind of fish | A tall tower by the shore
exercise: «It's Baltic out there!» said Sam, as snow blew past the door. What does Baltic mean? | A sea in northern Europe | Full of salt water | *Freezing cold | A shiny yellow stone
exercise: Which of these is one of the Baltic states? | Spain | Italy | *Estonia | Norway
test: Which phrase names a place, not the weather? | It's Baltic in here | Baltic weather today | A Baltic morning, very grey | *the Baltic Sea
test: Which of these is a Baltic language? | *Latvian | Greek | Italian | Spanish
exam: When Baltic is a name, such as the Baltic Sea, what must it start with? | *a capital letter | a full stop | an apostrophe | a number
